# Functional test: a Child SA rekey REFUSED on the REAL XFRM dataplane.
# VALIDATES: spec-fixit-child-rekey-answer-vs-installed-selectors AC-2 and AC-4 -- a peer
#            proposes a scope that no longer covers the SA in use, RFC 7296 Section 2.9.2
#            leaves no legal answer, and the responder refuses with TS_UNACCEPTABLE. The
#            kernel policy selectors stay exactly what the tunnel announced, and no
#            replacement state pair is ever installed.
# PREVENTS: the divergence the spec names. Before the fix, respondChildRekey answered the
#           intersection of the peer's narrowed proposal while newRekeyedChild installed
#           the retired pair's selectors, so the wire said 192.0.2.96/27 and the kernel
#           held 192.0.2.64/26. Traffic in the difference is protected at one end and
#           dropped at the other, with no notification and no log line.
#
# WHY THE ROLES ARE CROSSED. RFC 7296 Section 1.3.2 lets EITHER end start a
# CREATE_CHILD_SA, so the exchange role is not the IKE SA role. peer-a INITIATED the IKE
# SA and holds a long ESP lifetime, peer-b responded and holds a short one, so peer-b's
# soft expiry makes it drive the rekey that peer-a answers. This is the same crossing
# test/ipsec/ipsec-child-rekey-xfrm.ci uses, and for the same reason: the floor peer-a
# compares against is stored in the IKE_AUTH orientation and has to be swapped into this
# exchange's, so an orientation error here refuses every rekey rather than this one.
#
# WHY ONE DATAPLANE AND NOT TWO. The kernel keys an XFRM state on (src, dst, spi, proto)
# and the two ends of one negotiated Child SA are the SAME two states, so a second
# XfrmStateAdd answers EEXIST. peer-a therefore runs the real xfrm backend and peer-b the
# noop one, and every kernel assertion below is about peer-a.
#
# WHY peer-b NEEDS ze_test_ike_rekey_ts_local. A Ze peer proposes sa.PeerCfg at its rekey
# (proposeChildTSPayloads, engine/rekey.go), and startPeerSession copies that config when
# the session starts. An operator who narrows a peer's selectors now RESTARTS that peer
# (peerConfigChanged, engine/reconcile.go), which is what RFC 7296 Section 2.9.2 asks for,
# so the narrowed set travels on a fresh IKE_SA_INIT and the old scope is gone before
# anything proposes against it. A live SA therefore still proposes selectors that cover the
# scope it installed, and two Ze daemons alone still cannot reach Section 2.9.2's case
# where "the policy was changed in a way such that the currently used SA is against the
# policy". The override narrows the local half of THIS request alone, which is the peer
# that section describes. Production sets no such key (engine/testport.go,
# narrowedRekeyPairs).
#
# WHY THE SELECTORS CARRY A PORT. A prefix pair is symmetric under an orientation error:
# swapping the two halves of a policy whose only asymmetry is the prefix produces the
# INBOUND policy, which is installed anyway. A port on one side alone is what makes the
# swap visible, so the selector is TCP with a source port on peer-a's half.
#
# WHY THESE PREFIXES. The policies are real and node-wide for the life of the test, so
# they name RFC 5737 documentation addresses no traffic can be using. They are quarters of
# TEST-NET-1, distinct as printed strings from the TEST-NET-1 halves
# ipsec-child-rekey-xfrm.ci installs and from the TEST-NET-2/3 pair
# ipsec-teardown-leaves-nothing.ci installs, so each XFRM fixture reads only its own rows.
#
# Gated option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin: peer-a programs real XFRM state, so this SKIPs
# on a host without CAP_NET_ADMIN (the unprivileged dev box) and runs as root in the QEMU
# VM, where `fsuite ipsec` in scripts/evidence/qemu-all-tests.sh executes the suite.

option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
# Serialize against every other real-XFRM ipsec test. `ip xfrm state` and
# `ip xfrm policy` are NODE-WIDE, so a sibling running concurrently adds SPIs and
# policies this test's readers cannot tell from its own. MEASURED 2026-08-22: run
# together, ipsec-child-rekey-xfrm read the narrowing test's replacement SPIs as its
# own and reported POLICY-MOVED, while ipsec-child-rekey-xfrm-narrowing read this
# test's replacement SPIs and reported REKEY-ACCEPTED. Both verdicts were about the
# other test's kernel state.
option=exclusive:group=ipsec-xfrm
option=env:var=ze_test_ike_port:value=$PORT2
option=timeout:value=120s
option=env:var=ze_log_ike:value=info

tmpfs=refused.py:mode=755:terminator=EOF_REFUSED
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Watch a refused Child SA rekey in the kernel rather than in a log line.

Two facts are read from `ip xfrm`. The tunnel's state pair arrives, and from then on
NOTHING moves: no replacement pair joins it, and the policy selectors stay where the
IKE_AUTH exchange put them. A rekey peer-a answered instead of refusing would install a
second pair beside the first, because that is what make-before-break does.

The refusal itself is asserted on the daemons' log by the expect=stderr lines at the end
of the .ci file. This script owns the kernel half, which is what AC-4 asks for: the
policy selector the kernel holds equals the set the tunnel announced.
"""
import subprocess
import sys

from ze_api import wait_until

# The two halves of the negotiated selector, as `ip xfrm policy` prints them. Every policy
# this tunnel installs names one of them, and nothing else on the box does.
LOCAL_NET = "192.0.2.0/26"
REMOTE_NET = "192.0.2.64/26"

# The tunnel endpoints. An XFRM state row names them, so reading only those rows keeps a
# tunnel another fixture installed out of this one's arithmetic.
ENDPOINTS = frozenset(("127.0.0.1", "127.0.0.2"))

# The watch, in polls of a quarter second. peer-b's ESP lifetime is 10 seconds, so its
# rekey starts around 5 seconds after the pair arrives and its hard expiry lands at 10.
# 80 polls is 20 seconds, which covers both and stops well before peer-a could notice a
# peer that is no longer answering.
WATCH_POLLS = 80
WATCH_DELAY = 0.25


def run(*cmd):
    out = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if out.returncode != 0:
        sys.exit(f"{' '.join(cmd)} failed: {out.stderr.strip()}")
    return out.stdout


def spis():
    """The ESP SPIs of the states between this test's two endpoints.

    A state row opens with its `src <addr> dst <addr>` line and the SPI follows on the
    next line, so the endpoints are carried forward until the next row opens.
    """
    found = []
    endpoints = None
    for line in run("ip", "xfrm", "state").splitlines():
        fields = line.split()
        if not fields:
            continue
        if fields[0] == "src" and len(fields) >= 4:
            endpoints = frozenset((fields[1], fields[3]))
            continue
        if endpoints != ENDPOINTS:
            continue
        for i, field in enumerate(fields):
            if field == "spi" and i + 1 < len(fields):
                found.append(fields[i + 1])
    return frozenset(found)


def policies():
    """This tunnel's policy selectors, one normalised string per policy.

    The selector line carries the addresses, the protocol and the ports; the `dir` line
    that follows says which direction it is. A port that changed sides, a prefix the rekey
    moved, or a policy that was removed changes this set.
    """
    found = []
    selector = None
    for line in run("ip", "xfrm", "policy").splitlines():
        fields = line.split()
        if not fields:
            continue
        if fields[0] == "src":
            selector = " ".join(fields) if (LOCAL_NET in fields or REMOTE_NET in fields) else None
        elif selector is not None and fields[0] == "dir":
            found.append(f"{selector} | {fields[1]}")
            selector = None
    return frozenset(found)


if not wait_until(lambda: len(spis()) >= 2 and len(policies()) >= 2, attempts=400, delay=0.25):
    sys.exit(f"the tunnel never reached the kernel: states={sorted(spis())} policies={sorted(policies())}")
first_spis, first_policies = spis(), policies()
print("INSTALLED:", " ".join(sorted(first_spis)))
print("POLICIES:", " | ".join(sorted(first_policies)))

# The refusal, measured as the absence of every change a rekey makes. peer-b proposes
# 192.0.2.96/27, which covers no pair of the scope in use, so peer-a answers
# TS_UNACCEPTABLE and installs nothing. The first result recorded wins, and the poll ends
# on it.
moved = {}


def changed():
    current = spis()
    if current != first_spis:
        moved["states"] = sorted(current)
        return True
    current = policies()
    if current != first_policies:
        moved["policies"] = sorted(current)
        return True
    return False


wait_until(changed, attempts=WATCH_POLLS, delay=WATCH_DELAY)

if "policies" in moved:
    print("POLICY-MOVED: the refused rekey changed the installed selectors")
    print("  before:", " | ".join(sorted(first_policies)))
    print("  after: ", " | ".join(moved["policies"]))
    sys.exit(1)
if moved.get("states"):
    print("REKEY-ACCEPTED: peer-a installed states for a proposal that covers no pair of the scope in use")
    print("  before:", " ".join(sorted(first_spis)))
    print("  after: ", " ".join(moved["states"]))
    sys.exit(1)
if "states" in moved:
    # Every state left and none arrived. peer-b's Child SA hard-expired without a
    # replacement, which is the end state a refused rekey reaches.
    print("RETIRED:", " ".join(sorted(first_spis)))
print("POLICY-STABLE:", " | ".join(sorted(first_policies)))
EOF_REFUSED

# ── peer-b (background, noop dataplane, SHORT esp lifetime): binds 127.0.0.2 ──
# It responds to the IKE SA and drives the Child SA rekey. Its dataplane is the noop one
# so exactly one daemon owns what the kernel holds. Its configured selectors match
# peer-a's, so IKE_AUTH negotiates the full scope; only its REKEY proposal is narrowed.
stdin=peer-b:terminator=EOF_PEER_B
vpn {
	ipsec {
		esp-group esp-default {
			lifetime 10
			proposal 1 {
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		ike-group ike-default {
			key-exchange ikev2
			lifetime 28800
			proposal 1 {
				dh-group 14
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		site-to-site {
			peer peer-a {
				ike-group ike-default
				esp-group esp-default
				connection-type respond
				local-address 127.0.0.2
				remote-address 127.0.0.1
				mode tunnel
				traffic-selector 1 {
					protocol 6
					local {
						prefix 192.0.2.64/26
					}
					remote {
						prefix 192.0.2.0/26
						port 179
					}
				}
				authentication {
					mode pre-shared-secret
					pre-shared-secret test-key-1234
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_PEER_B

# ── peer-a (background, REAL xfrm, LONG esp lifetime): binds 127.0.0.1 ──
# It initiates the IKE SA, so sa.IsInitiator is true here while peer-b initiates the
# CREATE_CHILD_SA. That is the crossed case named at the top of this file.
stdin=peer-a:terminator=EOF_PEER_A
vpn {
	ipsec {
		esp-group esp-default {
			lifetime 3600
			proposal 1 {
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		ike-group ike-default {
			key-exchange ikev2
			lifetime 28800
			proposal 1 {
				dh-group 14
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		site-to-site {
			peer peer-b {
				ike-group ike-default
				esp-group esp-default
				connection-type initiate
				local-address 127.0.0.1
				remote-address 127.0.0.2
				mode tunnel
				traffic-selector 1 {
					protocol 6
					local {
						prefix 192.0.2.0/26
						port 179
					}
					remote {
						prefix 192.0.2.64/26
					}
				}
				authentication {
					mode pre-shared-secret
					pre-shared-secret test-key-1234
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_PEER_A

# peer-b's dataplane and its narrowed rekey proposal, and ONLY peer-b's: option=env
# applies to every process the runner launches, so the per-daemon overrides ride in front
# of the command. `env` rather than a shell, because env just execs and the daemon still
# gets the signal cmd=stop sends. 192.0.2.96/27 sits inside peer-b's configured
# 192.0.2.64/26 and does not cover it, which is the whole point: the proposal covers no
# pair of the scope in use.
cmd=background:seq=1:exec=env ze_test_ike_dataplane=noop ze_test_ike_rekey_ts_local=192.0.2.96/27 ze -:stdin=peer-b:name=peer-b
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=peer-a:name=peer-a
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=python3 refused.py:timeout=110s
# signal=term, not the default kill: peer-a's policies are REAL and node-wide, and the
# release that removes them runs on the way out of runEngine. A SIGKILLed peer-a leaves
# them in the kernel for the rest of the suite, where ipsec-teardown-leaves-nothing.ci
# asserts the XFRM tables are empty.
cmd=stop:seq=4:name=peer-a:signal=term
cmd=stop:seq=5:name=peer-b:signal=term

# NO engine-steps executor and no command= step, deliberately, for the reason
# ipsec-child-rekey-xfrm.ci records: the executor exits the moment its steps pass and the
# daemon carrying it exits with it, while every assertion here is about a kernel peer-a
# must still be running to hold.
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=INSTALLED:
expect=stdout:contains=POLICY-STABLE:
# peer-a refused, and named both selector sets so an operator can act (engine/inbound.go
# logs the error respondChildRekey returned). Without the refusal peer-a answers the
# intersection and this line never appears.
expect=stderr:contains=the rekey proposal narrows the scope in use
# peer-b's Child SA reached its hard lifetime with no replacement. A rekey peer-a had
# answered would have reset that lifetime, so this line is the peer's own statement that
# the exchange never completed.
expect=stderr:contains=hard lifetime expired
reject=stdout:pattern=POLICY-MOVED
reject=stdout:pattern=REKEY-ACCEPTED
reject=stderr:pattern=panic recovered
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
